{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 167 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "51c86f05050de62dbfa369f8b5f9cad22cbed8a74d33a66fa8182a6ef678d430" :edb/db-before-t 166 :edb/db-after-t 167 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533479 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:46:45.702Z" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399745 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186068867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399752 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399863 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186078574 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399884 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186083313 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399780 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399794 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399870 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399877 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399856 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399801 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399808 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186087859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399787 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399836 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096630 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399843 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093824 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186096980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399850 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1071 "![](images/27/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = g^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186096980 1071 "![](images/27/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186096980 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points." 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186101001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399891 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399822 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186110659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399691 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186115258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399698 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186119821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399719 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186119821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399726 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399642 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399656 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186119844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399635 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121247 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186124780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399733 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1071 "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186124780 1071 "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186124780 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186129123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399815 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186129124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399829 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186138484 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399649 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186147460 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399663 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186166865 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399904 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399684 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163594 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186167091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399898 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{y - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y^{2}}$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186167091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186167091 1074 "![](images/7b/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399705 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399712 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399759 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399670 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176105 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399677 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173944 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186176286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399739 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186176286 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 false] [17592186176286 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399766 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399773 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399636 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1069 "q-108368" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2t \\\\ y=-3+4t \\\\ z=5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:3x+2y-z=33.\n$$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1072 #uuid "3ee9813a-b1b3-5ef0-86f7-ac30b2000775" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399635 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y-z & =33 \\\\ 3(4+2t)+2(-3+4t)-(5t) & =33 \\\\ 12+6t-6+8t-5t & =33 \\\\ 9t & =27 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2(3)=10 \\\\ y=-3+4(3)=9 \\\\ z=5(3)=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(10,9,15).$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399637 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399638 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399639 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399640 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399641 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1077 "selection" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399641 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399636 1080 #uuid "f4bd1c19-a96b-51b8-b83f-2c4bebba5b26" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399637 1081 "(20,29,40)" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399637 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399637 1084 #uuid "3cbd933f-3f29-5070-8231-31ef77e6f4c5" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399638 1081 "(-6,-23,-25)" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399638 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399638 1084 #uuid "f073d53a-a8e7-5ae2-82c8-e5cb31403d9c" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399639 1081 "(2,-7,-5)" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399639 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399639 1084 #uuid "654434c9-d7c4-5e1e-bfe8-aaafc9425c76" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399640 1081 "No intersection" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399640 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399640 1084 #uuid "509a688f-9e49-51a5-95ba-17bf03e47002" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399641 1081 "(10,9,15)" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399641 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399641 1084 #uuid "d1ec3fa5-fe3a-5ef1-bb6e-18d2712b8636" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399643 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1069 "q-108371" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨10,-5,12⟩+s⟨4,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,5⟩.\n$$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1072 #uuid "0da8771f-0983-5e77-be97-afe167de38df" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399642 1074 "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vector to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨4,3,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =15i-20j+8k \\\\  & =⟨15,-20,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =⟨10,-5,12⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ ⟨4+5w,-2-w,3+2w⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ 15(4+5w)-20(-2-w)+8(3+2w) & =346 \\\\ 60+75w+40+20w+24+16w & =346 \\\\ 111w & =222 \\\\ w & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨4+5(2),-2-(2),3+2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14,-4,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(14,-4,7).$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399644 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399645 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399646 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399643 1075 