![](images/20/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399843 1072 #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399843 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399845 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399846 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399847 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399848 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399849 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1077 "selection" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399848 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399844 1080 #uuid "989f84dd-5950-58e9-b6c6-2ad30ae07799" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399845 1081 "$x=-2$ and $x=1$ only" 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399845 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533479 17592186045585 true] [17592186399845 1084 #uuid "13fdeb0d-d904-56f6-8b96-e74fb1d0181b" 13194139533479 175921860